     1	#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
     2	# /// script
     3	# requires-python = ">=3.11"
     4	# dependencies = ["mcp>=2.0.0", "uvicorn"]
     5	# ///
     6	"""xcode-mcp-front daemon.
     7	
     8	Problem: Xcode's MCP-connection "Allow" prompt is keyed per connecting PID. Every
     9	separate process that execs `xcrun mcpbridge` (one per kicker node, one per Claude
    10	Code / Codex session) makes Xcode ask again. kicker runs many concurrent nodes, so
    11	that's many concurrent popups for the same underlying tool.
    12	
    13	Fix: hold exactly ONE `xcrun mcpbridge` child process for the whole life of this
    14	daemon, and front it with a Streamable HTTP MCP server. Every downstream client
    15	(kicker nodes, Claude Code, Codex — anything that can speak MCP-over-HTTP) points
    16	at this daemon's URL instead of spawning mcpbridge itself. Xcode approves ONE PID,
    17	once, and stays approved as long as this daemon (and its mcpbridge child) keep running.
    18	
    19	Runs in one of two modes, chosen by which env vars are set (see below):
    20	  - SINGLE-upstream (default, what's been running since 2026-08-14): fronts just
    21	    one upstream (normally mcpbridge). Dumb passthrough — tool names forwarded
    22	    unprefixed, exactly as the upstream advertises them. This is the deployed
    23	    xcode-mcp-front daemon; nothing about its behavior changes in this mode.
    24	  - MULTI-upstream (XCODE_MCP_FRONT_UPSTREAMS set): fronts several upstreams
    25	    behind the SAME endpoint, tool names prefixed per-upstream
    26	    (`<name>__<tool>`, e.g. `xcode__BuildProject`, `drews__run_project_unmonitored`)
    27	    so two upstreams that happen to share a tool name can't collide or shadow
    28	    each other. Built for exactly one reason: Drew's xcode-mcp
    29	    (`drews-xcode-mcp`) has no approval-dialog friction on its own — a client can
    30	    already spawn `uvx drews-xcode-mcp` directly with zero setup, no wrapper
    31	    needed — so it never got its own standalone daemon (built, verified, then
    32	    torn back down, 2026-08-14 — see git history). But "one endpoint with BOTH
    33	    Xcode tools available, prefixed so there's no confusion" is a real,
    34	    different thing worth having alongside the single-upstream daemon, not
    35	    instead of it. Both modes share every line of connection/click/reconnect
    36	    logic below via the Upstream class — no duplicated logic between them.
    37	
    38	This is a dumb passthrough, not a real aggregator: it forwards list_tools/call_tool
    39	to the relevant upstream ClientSession(s) and returns whatever comes back. It does
    40	not interpret tool semantics. Calls to a given upstream are serialized through
    41	that upstream's own lock (tolerance for concurrent overlapping calls hasn't been
    42	tested, so this starts correctness-first — tool calls are already human-paced, so
    43	serialization shouldn't be felt in practice); different upstreams' calls are
    44	fully independent of each other.
    45	
    46	VALIDATED 2026-08-14 (all live, against a real Xcode):
    47	- One connection tolerates many sequential calls of different shapes, no reconnect
    48	  needed (spike.py, then a real multi-call session through this daemon).
    49	- Quitting Xcode does NOT crash this process — it keeps serving HTTP, but the
    50	  mcpbridge child dies and every call after that fails clean with "Connection
    51	  closed".
    52	- The approval prompt's PID (confirmed from the actual dialog, not assumed) is
    53	  THIS daemon's own PID, not the ephemeral mcpbridge child's — so as long as this
    54	  process keeps running, reconnecting mcpbridge in a loop should NOT need a fresh
    55	  approval each time, only the very first connection (or after a genuine
    56	  permission reset) does.
    57	- The approval prompt is requested LAZILY, on the first list_tools call, not at
    58	  the initial connect — the per-upstream reconnect loop polls for it (and
    59	  clicks it) continuously in the connected steady-state too, not just while
    60	  reconnecting.
    61	- Xcode's approval dialogs do NOT stack — only one shows at a time, and a stale
    62	  unanswered one blocks the next (including this daemon's own) from appearing.
    63	  The click logic reads each dialog's own PID: clicks Allow for our own live
    64	  pid, clicks Don't Allow for a dead pid (nobody's waiting on it — a plain
    65	  os.kill(pid,0) liveness check, no PID-history file needed), leaves any other
    66	  live pid's dialog strictly alone.
    67	- Drew's xcode-mcp (drews-xcode-mcp) has NO approval-dialog behavior at all —
    68	  folder-allowlist auth (XCODEMCP_ALLOWED_FOLDERS / --allowed) instead of a
    69	  live per-PID popup. Its Upstream instance just never finds a dialog to click;
    70	  harmless no-op, no special-casing needed.
    71	
    72	Env overrides — SINGLE-upstream mode (default):
    73	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_UPSTREAM_CMD    default "xcrun"
    74	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_UPSTREAM_ARGS   default "mcpbridge" (space-separated)
    75	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_REQUIRE_XCODE   default "1" — wait for Xcode.app before connecting.
    76	                                   Set "0" for an upstream (like drews-xcode-mcp)
    77	                                   that doesn't need a live Xcode process at all.
    78	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_AUTO_ALLOW      default "1" — click-Allow-automatically. Off
    79	                                   switch for anyone who'd rather approve by hand.
    80	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_SERVER_NAME     default "xcode-mcp-front"
    81	
    82	Env overrides — MULTI-upstream mode (mutually exclusive with the single-upstream
    83	UPSTREAM_CMD/ARGS/REQUIRE_XCODE vars above — set THIS instead):
    84	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_MCP_INFO        path to a _mcp_info.json in the Claude Code shape
    85	                                   ({"mcpServers": {"<name>": {"command", "args"}}}),
    86	                                   plus per-server "prefix" (default "<name>__") and
    87	                                   "quirks" (["require_xcode"] for an upstream that
    88	                                   needs a live Xcode). See mcp_config.py, which
    89	                                   validates it and REJECTS fields the daemon does
    90	                                   not pass through (env, cwd, url, ...) by name.
    91	                                   Tool names are prefixed in the merged tool list
    92	                                   and un-prefixed again when routing a call back.
    93	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_UPSTREAMS       REPLACED by the file above; setting it is a
    94	                                   startup error naming the replacement. The old
    95	                                   colon/comma format corrupted a command path
    96	                                   containing ':' and an argument containing ','.
    97	
    98	Env overrides — both modes:
    99	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_HOST             default "127.0.0.1" (do not bind wider — no auth layer)
   100	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_PORT             default "8765"
   101	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_XCODE_APP_PATH   default "/Applications/Xcode.app" — the reconnect
   102	                                    loop matches THIS exact binary path, not a bare
   103	                                    process name (Xcode.app and Xcode-beta.app share
   104	                                    both their process name and bundle ID, so only
   105	                                    the full path tells them apart — confirmed live)
   106	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_RECONNECT_POLL_S default "5" (seconds between reconnect attempts)
   107	  XCODE_MCP_FRONT_CONNECT_TIMEOUT_S default "15" (per-attempt connect timeout, so a
   108	                                    stuck approval dialog can't hang a whole attempt
   109	                                    forever — it just times out and the next tick
   110	                                    tries the click again)
   111	"""
   112	
   113	import asyncio
   114	import contextlib
   115	import logging
   116	import os
   117	import subprocess
   118	import time
   119	
   120	import anyio
   121	import uvicorn
   122	
   123	import mcp_config
   124	from mcp import types
   125	from mcp.client.session import ClientSession
   126	from mcp.client.stdio import StdioServerParameters, stdio_client
   127	from mcp.server.lowlevel import Server
