codex
I read all six required files in full, plus `ROADMAP.md`, `SPEC.md`, `lib.sh`, and the installed MCP Python SDK 2.1.1 exception path. I could not run the shell suite because the sandbox forbids `mktemp`, which `lib.sh` requires. I did run read-only construction probes against the installed SDK.

1. **High. A known-broken upstream remains “connected” until teardown completes, and a wedged teardown can eventually restart the entire aggregator.** `list_tools()` and `call_tool()` only test whether `self.session is None` ([daemon.py:481](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:481), [daemon.py:516](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:516)). The heartbeat sets `known_broken` before leaving the nested context managers ([daemon.py:656](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:656)), but `self.session` is cleared only after those managers exit ([daemon.py:673](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:673)). The file already documents that this teardown can hang permanently ([daemon.py:547](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:547)); the watchdog then calls `os._exit(75)` ([daemon.py:571](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:571)). I infer this scenario: one upstream fails its heartbeat, teardown hangs, later lists keep entering the broken session, and after 180 seconds the watchdog drops every healthy upstream by exiting the shared daemon. Clear the public session reference as soon as `known_broken` is set, and make both operations reject `known_broken`; preferably restart or kill only the defective child instead of exiting the aggregate process.

2. **High. A client can cache a partial surface permanently after an upstream recovers.** A disconnected upstream is omitted and the partial result is returned normally ([daemon.py:746](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:746), [daemon.py:760](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:760)). The server registers no tool-change notification path ([daemon.py:837](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:837), [daemon.py:847](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:847)). A construction probe against the installed SDK returned `tools.listChanged: false`, which follows from its default `NotificationOptions()` and capability derivation ([server.py:523](/Users/jonathan/.cache/uv/environments-v2/daemon-411cce993762ffaa/lib/python3.14/site-packages/mcp/server/lowlevel/server.py:523), [server.py:580](/Users/jonathan/.cache/uv/environments-v2/daemon-411cce993762ffaa/lib/python3.14/site-packages/mcp/server/lowlevel/server.py:580)). I infer this scenario: a client initializes while Xcode is unavailable, lists and caches only Drew’s tools, Xcode reconnects, and that session never lists again because the server advertised that the list does not change. Ship degraded listing together with downstream change notification, or force affected downstream sessions to reconnect when availability changes.

3. **High for Phase 2. Prefix fallback will bypass a deny list and preserve renamed tools under their old names.** Every dispatch miss is routed solely by its configured prefix ([daemon.py:770](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:770)). I infer the Phase 2 failure directly from that path: `xcode__SwitchScheme` is removed from the published dispatch table, but a caller that remembers the name still reaches Xcode through prefix fallback. The same path makes a Phase 3 source name callable after it was replaced by a mapped name. Remove direct prefix fallback; if calls before the first list must work, refresh the relevant upstream catalog and require an exact dispatch hit before forwarding.

4. **High. A stale dispatch entry converts a valid upstream “unknown tool” response into a false disconnection and deliberately breaks the healthy connection.** The table changes only after a downstream `tools/list` ([daemon.py:716](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:716), [daemon.py:758](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:758)). `Upstream.call_tool()` catches every exception, marks the connection broken, and returns `None` ([daemon.py:519](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:519)); its caller reports “not connected right now” ([daemon.py:796](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:796)). In SDK 2.1.1, an upstream JSON-RPC error is raised as `MCPError` ([jsonrpc_dispatcher.py:443](/Users/jonathan/.cache/uv/environments-v2/daemon-411cce993762ffaa/lib/python3.14/site-packages/mcp/shared/jsonrpc_dispatcher.py:443)). I infer this scenario: an upstream reconnects without tool `old`, the stale table still routes `prefix__old`, the upstream returns `-32602`, and the daemon tears down a working session while telling the client to retry. Keep a per-upstream catalog generation, replace it after each successful snapshot, and treat application-level `MCPError` responses as call errors rather than transport breakage.

5. **High. Pagination has a time bound but no structural bound, and one bad upstream delays every later upstream.** The drain records neither visited cursors nor page or tool counts ([daemon.py:489](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:489)). Its only bound is the shared 600-second call timeout ([daemon.py:491](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:491)), and aggregate listing visits upstreams sequentially ([daemon.py:746](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:746)). I infer this scenario: an upstream returns cursor sequence `A, B, A, B...` with one tool per fast page; memory grows until timeout or process failure, and healthy upstreams after it are never queried during that interval. Add repeated-cursor detection plus deliberate page/tool ceilings, use a shorter discovery timeout, and query independent upstreams concurrently. The single outer `fail_after` is the correct deadline shape because a per-page timeout alone would permit an infinite sequence of fast pages.

6. **Medium. The heartbeat is not starved by the pagination lock; it bypasses the lock and can overlap the supposedly serialized request.** Client-facing list and call operations acquire `self.lock` ([daemon.py:480](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:480), [daemon.py:515](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:515)), but the heartbeat calls `session.list_tools()` directly ([daemon.py:641](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:641)). This contradicts the file’s claim that all calls to one upstream are serialized ([daemon.py:40](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:40)). I infer that a client pagination drain and heartbeat can concurrently list through the same upstream session, despite the stated reason for the lock. Route heartbeat through the same request-serialization mechanism, or document and test concurrent upstream requests before removing that guarantee.

7. **Medium. Cursor refusal is permitted, but `ValueError` produces the wrong protocol error and an empty cursor is accepted.** The handler rejects only truthy cursors and raises raw `ValueError` ([daemon.py:739](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:739)). Therefore `cursor: ""` is treated as no cursor. Under the test’s 2025 protocol, SDK 2.1.1 converts an unclassified exception to JSON-RPC code `0` with the message preserved ([jsonrpc_dispatcher.py:751](/Users/jonathan/.cache/uv/environments-v2/daemon-411cce993762ffaa/lib/python3.14/site-packages/mcp/shared/jsonrpc_dispatcher.py:751)); under the modern path, it becomes generic internal error and loses the diagnostic ([runner.py:534](/Users/jonathan/.cache/uv/environments-v2/daemon-411cce993762ffaa/lib/python3.14/site-packages/mcp/server/runner.py:534)). The live protocol specification says invalid cursors should return `-32602`, so refusal itself is valid but the current shape is not ([MCP pagination specification](https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2025-06-18/server/utilities/pagination)). Raise `MCPError(INVALID_PARAMS, ...)` when `params.cursor is not None`.

8. **Medium. The initialization and failure instructions still describe an Xcode-specific proxy and can contradict the configured surface.** Single-upstream mode always claims it fronts Apple’s bridge ([daemon.py:803](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:803)); multi-upstream mode calls every server “Xcode-adjacent” and says prefixes equal upstream names ([daemon.py:826](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:826)). Configuration explicitly permits a custom prefix ([mcp_config.py:77](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/mcp_config.py:77)), and every unavailable-call response says Xcode is being checked and its dialog clicked ([daemon.py:695](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:695)). A read-only probe with upstream `alpha`, prefix `custom__`, reproduced instructions telling the client to use `alpha__`. Generate generic instructions from configured prefixes and quirks, and mention Xcode only for upstreams carrying `require_xcode`.

9. **Medium for Phase 2. Unknown root-level configuration keys are silently ignored.** Server-entry keys are validated exhaustively ([mcp_config.py:59](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/mcp_config.py:59)), but `load()` extracts only `mcpServers` and never validates the enclosing object’s other keys ([mcp_config.py:107](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/mcp_config.py:107)). I infer this scenario: a valid `mcpServers` object sits beside misspelled root stanza `"blocks"` or `"toolMaps"`; validation succeeds and the limiting policy never applies. Define and test the allowed root schema before adding the sieve and map, so misspelled policy stanzas fail visibly.

10. **Medium. The daemon test does not prove two of its strongest claims.** The cursor assertion checks only for the substring `error` ([test-mcp-front-daemon.sh:94](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/tests/test-mcp-front-daemon.sh:94)), so code `0`, `-32603`, or an unrelated handler failure can pass. The “connected-but-empty” assertion checks only that `delta__` is absent ([test-mcp-front-daemon.sh:150](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/tests/test-mcp-front-daemon.sh:150)); the same assertion passes if delta never connected. A concrete false-green scenario is that both gamma and delta fail to launch while alpha connects: every current degraded-surface assertion still passes. Assert HTTP/JSON-RPC status, exact `-32602`, and the cursor diagnostic; add observable stub readiness or a separate probe that fails when the empty stub never connected.

The prefix-set validation is correct for the current fallback router. Duplicate prefixes, nested prefixes, and an empty prefix beside another upstream are rejected before startup ([mcp_config.py:115](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/mcp_config.py:115)), and the sorted adjacent comparison is sufficient because strings sharing a prefix are contiguous in lexical order. Rejecting `env`, `cwd`, and `url` is also correct for this implementation because `StdioServerParameters` currently receives only command and args ([daemon.py:588](/Users/jonathan/svnCheckouts/js-db-ad-astra/tools/xcode-mcp-front/daemon.py:588)); accepting those fields now would claim behavior the daemon does not provide.
